---
title: The poweruser endgame
date: 2026-08-19
---
I often wonder what the endgame for developers and engineers are. I know, even of many of the people who truly are in
this field for their love thereof, it's not much beyond a job at the end of the day.

So what about power users? People, like myself, who love optimizing hell out of everything they do? More often than not,
those who maintain a dotfiles repo, maybe use Nix, build their own personal library functions, etc...

Looking back, most of my side projects aren't really "projects" so much as complaints about my own workflow that got out
of hand.

### The ramifications

The obvious problem is that this doesn't scale. Every hour spent patching your WM or rewriting your editor's
fuzzy-finder is an hour not spent on whatever you were actually supposed to be doing. Yak-shaving is a very real, very
seductive trap, and I've fallen into it more times than I'd like to admit.

The less obvious benefit, though, is depth. Once you've written your own editor, your build system stops being a black
box. Once you've patched your WM, X11/Wayland internals stop being magic. You end up understanding the *entire* stack
you live in, top to bottom, instead of just the surface area most people ever touch.

I am genuinely someone who loves understanding as much of my tools as I can, so doing all of this in a "non-product"
kind of way, and one which is _just_ for me, feels great.